In a C program, you can use the `exit()` function to terminate the program explicitly. The `exit()` function takes an integer argument, which can be used to set the exit status of the program. Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *ptr;
    int value = 42;

    ptr = (int *) malloc(sizeof(int));
    if (ptr == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed!\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    *ptr = value;
    free(ptr);

    printf("The value is %d\n", *ptr);
    exit(0);
}
```

In this example, `exit(1)` is used to terminate the program with an exit status of 1 when memory allocation fails. The `exit(0)` function is used to terminate the program with an exit status of 0 when the program runs successfully.

You can also let the operating system terminate the program by not including any explicit `exit()` calls. For example, if the program encounters an uncaught signal or a segmentation fault, it will be terminated automatically.
